              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The colourability of a knot tells one information about its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+group"} knot group
            yet has a simple, and visually attractive aspect that seems almost to avoid all mention of groups, presentations, etc., except at a fairly naive level.
          %p
            The easiest form of colourability to examine is
            = succeed "-colourability." do
              %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
          %h2#colourability_2
            %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            \-colourability
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+diagram"} knot diagram
              is
              %em
                %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
                \-colourable
              if we can assign colours to its arcs such that
            %ol
              %li
                %p each arc is assigned one colour;
              %li
                %p
                  %em exactly
                  three colours are used in the assignment;
              %li
                %p at each crossing, either all the arcs have the same colour, or arcs of all three colours meet in the crossing.
