              %text#text2397{:style => "font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-align: start; line-height: 125%; fill: #000000; fill-opacity: 1; stroke: none; stroke-width: 1px; stroke-linecap: butt; stroke-linejoin: miter; stroke-opacity: 1; font-family: Times New Roman;", :x => "146.42857", "xml:space" => "preserve", :y => "178.73734"}
                %tspan#tspan2399{:x => "146.42857", :y => "178.73734"} b
          %p
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo +
                    %mi c
                    %msub
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi n
                    %mn 2
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a+c \equiv_n 2b
          %p What, of course, needs to be checked (left to ‘the reader’) is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                this notion is a generalisation of 3-colourability (i.e. coincides in the case of
                = succeed ");" do
                  %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
            %li
              %p
                %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-colourability is preserved under
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reidemeister+moves"} Reidemeister moves
                so can be applied to a knot, not just to a knot diagram;
          %p
            and then to find some examples of, say, 5-colourability. What knots are 5-colourable? Which
            = succeed "-" do
              %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,k)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus+knots"} torus knots
            are 5-colorable, and so on. This is not important mathematically, but is quite fun and, in fact, does give insight and experience in working with the Reidemeister moves.
          %h2#coloring_by_a_quandle Coloring by a quandle
          %p
            There is an even more general notion of coloring a knot
            %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            by the elements of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quandle"} quandle
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊳
                    %mo :
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Q
                    %mo →
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Q,\rhd : Q \times Q \to Q)
            %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            by
            %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            corresponds to a quandle homomorphism from the fundamental quandle
            %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q(K)
            of
            %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_41.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_42.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            (as in the above diagram), the identity
            %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_43.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊳
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c = a \rhd b
            must be respected. In particular,
            = succeed "-coloring" do
              %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_44.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math#mathml_97d871a90076ba9ee1a41044592d45e7c6b1d091_45.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
